---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { ConsentBanner } from '@/components/ConsentBanner';

// Image imports
import imgHero from '@/assets/Shoulder-pain-hero.webp?url';
import imgCauses from '@/assets/shoulder_pain_common_causes.png?url';
import imgChain from '@/assets/the_chain_neck_thoracic_spine_ribs_shouldergirdle_shoulder.png?url';
import imgPosture from '@/assets/open_scissors_hyperkyphosis.png?url';
import imgAssessment from '@/assets/range-of-motion-shoulder-assessment.webp?url';
import imgFooter from '@/assets/eva-andersson-portrait.webp?url';
import logo from '@/assets/logo.webp?url';
---

<BaseLayout 
  title="Shoulder Pain Treatment in North Vancouver | Scandinavian Clinic"
  description="Shoulder pain is not a single condition. The diagnosis matters because treatments diverge. Get a targeted assessment to find the root cause."
  robots="noindex"
>
  <div class="min-h-screen bg-background pt-20">
    
    <header class="bg-background border-b border-border fixed top-0 w-full z-50">
      <div class="container mx-auto px-4 h-20 flex items-center justify-between">
        <div class="flex items-center">
          <a href="https://scandinavianclinic.com">
            <img src={logo} alt="Scandinavian Clinic Logo" class="h-12 w-auto" />
          </a>
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-center gap-6">
          <a href="tel:+16049264883" class="hidden md:block text-foreground font-medium hover:text-primary transition-colors">(604) 926-4883</a>
          <a href="https://booking.scandinavianclinic.com" class="booking-link bg-primary text-primary-foreground px-4 py-2 rounded-md font-medium hover:bg-primary/90 transition-colors">Book Your Assessment</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </header>
    
    <main class="pt-24 pb-16">
      <div class="container mx-auto px-4 max-w-4xl">
        <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold text-foreground mb-8">Shoulder Pain Treatment in North Vancouver</h1>
        
        <p class="text-xl font-medium text-foreground mb-4"><strong>Quick Answer:</strong> "Shoulder pain" is not a single condition.</p>
        
        <p class="text-lg text-muted-foreground mb-4">At least a dozen distinct problems produce pain in roughly the same place, and which one you have determines what will help — a stiff, globally restricted shoulder needs the opposite handling from an overloaded rotator cuff. What they have in common is that the shoulder rarely fails on its own: it depends on the neck, the mid-back, the ribs, and the shoulder blade, and when those aren't doing their share, the shoulder takes the load.</p>
        
        <p class="text-lg text-muted-foreground mb-12">Shoulder pain narrows your life quietly. Reaching a top shelf, putting on a jacket, fastening a seatbelt, sleeping on that side — all become things you plan around. Often there was no injury. The ache crept in and then wouldn't leave.</p>
        
        <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none text-muted-foreground">
          
          <img src={imgHero} alt="The shoulder — pain at the front and outer shoulder, worse overhead, worse at night." class="w-full max-w-2xl mx-auto rounded-lg shadow-md my-10 bg-white" />

          <div class="my-8">
            <a href="https://booking.scandinavianclinic.com" class="booking-link inline-block bg-accent hover:bg-accent/90 text-accent-foreground font-medium py-3 px-6 rounded-md transition-colors mr-4">Book Your Assessment</a>
            <a href="https://scandinavianclinic.com" class="inline-block text-primary font-medium py-3 hover:underline">See all conditions Eva treats</a>
          </div>

          <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-foreground mb-4">Common symptoms:</h2>
          <ul class="space-y-2 mb-8">
            <li>Pain reaching overhead, out to the side, or behind your back</li>
            <li>Pain at night, especially lying on that side</li>
            <li>Weakness or a sense the arm won't do what you ask</li>
            <li>Catching, clicking, or a painful arc partway through the movement</li>
            <li>Stiffness that limits the movement itself, not just the comfort of it</li>
            <li>Aching that spreads into the upper arm</li>
          </ul>

          <hr class="my-12 border-border" />

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-foreground mb-6">When does shoulder pain need urgent attention?</h2>
          <p>Most shoulder pain is not an emergency. However, some situations need prompt medical care rather than physical therapy:</p>
          
          <div class="bg-red-50/50 dark:bg-red-950/20 border-l-4 border-red-500 p-6 rounded-r-xl my-8">
            <h4 class="text-lg font-bold text-foreground flex items-center gap-2 mb-2">🚨 Emergency Red Flags</h4>
            <p class="m-0 mb-4">Seek emergency medical care immediately if shoulder pain comes with chest pain, shortness of breath, sweating, or pain spreading to the jaw or left arm. Shoulder pain can occasionally signal a cardiac event.</p>
            <p class="m-0">Also seek prompt medical evaluation for sudden, significant weakness or inability to lift the arm after a fall, pain following major trauma, or a shoulder joint that appears visibly deformed.</p>
          </div>

          <p>Outside of these situations, shoulder pain that hasn't improved after a couple of weeks is a reasonable point to have it assessed.</p>

          <hr class="my-12 border-border" />

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-foreground mb-6">Shoulder pain is not one condition</h2>

          <img src={imgCauses} alt="The differential — a simple grid of the main problems that present as shoulder pain, grouped by whether movement is limited by pain or by stiffness." class="w-full max-w-2xl mx-auto rounded-lg shadow-md my-10 bg-white" />

          <p>The diagnosis matters here more than it does almost anywhere else in the body, because the treatments diverge. The problems that commonly present as shoulder pain include:</p>
          
          <ul class="space-y-2 mb-6">
            <li>Rotator cuff–related pain (an irritated, overloaded cuff)</li>
            <li>Rotator cuff tears (traumatic or age-related)</li>
            <li>Frozen shoulder (global stiffness)</li>
            <li>Osteoarthritis of the main shoulder joint</li>
            <li>Acromioclavicular (AC) joint problems at the tip of the shoulder</li>
            <li>Long head of biceps tendon pain at the front</li>
            <li>Instability and labral tears</li>
            <li>Calcific tendinopathy (a calcium deposit in the tendon, which can be dramatically painful)</li>
            <li>Referred pain from the neck (with a shoulder that examines and images normally)</li>
            <li>Scapular and thoracic movement problems (where the shoulder itself is structurally sound)</li>
            <li>Referred pain from internal organs</li>
          </ul>

          <p>The physical tests don't cleanly separate them on their own. Commonly used shoulder tests have reasonable sensitivity but poor specificity — the Hawkins-Kennedy test correctly identifies about 74% of affected shoulders, but only rules out 57% of unaffected ones; Neer's sign sits at 78% and 58% (Hegedus et al., 2012). A positive test tells you less than most people assume.</p>

          <p>This is also why research on shoulder pain is difficult to apply. A scoping review of studies on subacromial pain syndrome from 1972 to 2019 found no single recognized diagnostic criteria, concluding that studies are too varied to compare easily (Karel et al., 2023). When a trial reports that exercise helps shoulder pain, it is reporting an average across a mixed group of people whose underlying problems were not identical.</p>

          <p>So the useful question isn't which treatment works for shoulder pain in general. It's what is actually happening in your shoulder, and what is putting it under load.</p>

          <hr class="my-12 border-border" />

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-foreground mb-6">Why the rotator cuff gets overloaded</h2>

          <img src={imgChain} alt="The chain — neck, thoracic spine, ribs, shoulder blade, and shoulder." class="w-full max-w-2xl mx-auto rounded-lg shadow-md my-10 bg-white" />

          <p>Karel Lewit and the Prague School look beyond the shoulder joint itself. When a rotator cuff is irritated, it is usually the victim carrying extra load because another part of the system isn't doing its share.</p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-foreground mt-8 mb-4">1. Disrupted joint centration and shoulder rhythm</h3>
          <p>For your shoulder to move smoothly without pain, two things must work in precise teamwork:</p>
          <ul class="space-y-4 mb-6">
            <li><strong>Joint centration:</strong> The head of your arm bone must stay centered in its shallow socket throughout movement. If it drifts off-center, the bone rides upward and compresses the rotator cuff tendons above it.</li>
            <li><strong>Arm-and-blade rhythm (Scapulohumeral rhythm):</strong> As you raise your arm, your arm bone and shoulder blade move in a steady 2:1 ratio (2 degrees of arm movement for every 1 degree of shoulder blade rotation).</li>
          </ul>
          <p>When this timing breaks down — causing the blade to shrug, tip forward, or wing outward — the arm bone pinches the tissues inside the joint. Research shows that as shoulder rhythm becomes increasingly disrupted, pain levels directly rise. Retraining this shoulder-blade coordination alongside general exercises improves pain and function far more than exercise alone (Zhong et al., 2024).</p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-foreground mt-8 mb-4">2. A stiff mid-back and ribs</h3>
          <p>Your arm cannot reach fully overhead unless your mid-back extends and rotates to allow it. Restoring mid-back and rib mobility frequently improves overhead reach in the very same session, before anything has even been done to the shoulder itself.</p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-foreground mt-8 mb-4">3. Referred pain from the neck</h3>
          <p>Neck dysfunction commonly refers pain directly into the shoulder. A classic pattern is shoulder pain accompanied by restricted neck movement — where treating the neck relieves the shoulder. (Learn more on our <a href="/conditions/neck-pain-and-whiplash-treatment-north-vancouver" class="text-primary hover:underline">neck pain page</a>).</p>

          <hr class="my-12 border-border" />

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-foreground mb-6">Trunk Posture and Shoulders</h2>

          <img src={imgPosture} alt="Open scissors — the ribcage flared up and the pelvis tipped forward, the two planes diverging, with the shoulder blade's changed resting position marked." class="w-full max-w-2xl mx-auto rounded-lg shadow-md my-10 bg-white" />

          <p>As an example, Hyperkyphosis (an extreme forward curve of the upper back) causes shoulder problems by pulling your shoulder blades forward and down, which limits your arm movement, pinches your tendons, and leads to painful conditions like shoulder impingement.</p>

          <hr class="my-12 border-border" />

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-foreground mb-6">What about frozen shoulder?</h2>
          <p>"Frozen shoulder" is a widely used label rather than a precise structural diagnosis, but the presentation it describes is real and distinctive: movement is limited by global stiffness rather than only by pain, in every direction, with turning the arm outward (external rotation) usually the most restricted.</p>
          <p>It is often described as passing through three stages and resolving on its own within a year or two. However, a systematic review examining this natural history concluded that the theory of predictable recovery phases leading to complete resolution without treatment is unfounded (Wong et al., Physiotherapy, 2017) — a meaningful proportion of people experience stiffness or reduced reach years later.</p>

          <hr class="my-12 border-border" />

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-foreground mb-6">What about a scan or a rotator cuff tear?</h2>
          <p>Rotator cuff changes — including tears that cause no pain at all — are a normal part of getting older. A finding on an MRI report does not automatically prove it is the true source of your pain, nor does it decide treatment on its own.</p>
          <p>Where pain travels down the arm past the elbow, or comes with numbness, tingling, or weakness in the hand, that points toward a neck issue rather than a purely local shoulder problem — another key distinction evaluated during assessment.</p>

          <hr class="my-12 border-border" />

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-foreground mb-6">How Eva treats shoulder pain</h2>
          <p>What treatment looks like depends on what the assessment finds — a stiff shoulder, an overloaded cuff, and a shoulder problem driven by the neck require three completely different plans. The sequence below outlines the general approach:</p>

          <img src={imgAssessment} alt="Range of motion shoulder assessment" class="w-full max-w-2xl mx-auto rounded-lg shadow-md my-10 bg-white" />

          <ol class="list-decimal pl-5 space-y-4 mb-8">
            <li><strong>Establish the underlying issue:</strong> Neck movement, thoracic spine and rib mobility, shoulder motion during arm elevation, joint centration, trunk posture, and whether limitation is driven by pain or structural stiffness.</li>
            <li><strong>Soft tissue treatment for tight muscles:</strong> Massage, facia release, specific stretching, trigger point therapy, and targeted soft-tissue work.</li>
            <li><strong>Restore mobility:</strong> Specific mobilization of the thoracic spine, ribs, and neck, as well as the shoulder joint itself where restricted.</li>
            <li><strong>Retrain shoulder-blade coordination:</strong> Specific exercises to restore proper muscle patterns.</li>
            <li><strong>Weight-bearing through the arm:</strong> Dynamic Neuromuscular Stabilization uses positions from infant motor development — such as loading through the hands e.g. in four-point kneeling — before progressing to reaching overhead. These developmental positions promote shoulder blade and core coordination naturally without “overthinking” (Kobesová & Kolář, J Bodyw Mov Ther, 2014). <a href="https://scandinavianclinic.com" class="text-primary hover:underline">[Learn more about DNS →]</a></li>
            <li><strong>Progressive loading:</strong> Light rotator cuff activation, progressing toward the functional demands of your daily life, work, or sports. Structured exercise is the evidence-based first-line treatment for rotator cuff–related shoulder pain (Pieters et al., JOSPT, 2020).</li>
          </ol>

          <p>If you perform repetitive tasks — whether in sports like tennis, swimming, and climbing, or in daily work like computer use, painting, reaching, and heavy lifting — the issue is often a combination of factors: stiffness in the mid-back, limited shoulder mobility, and muscle compensation. Your specific reaching, lifting, or stroke mechanics are evaluated alongside the shoulder itself.</p>

          <hr class="my-12 border-border" />

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-foreground mb-6">What to expect</h2>
          <p>The timeline depends on which problem you have, and you will receive an honest estimate after your initial assessment:</p>
          <ul class="space-y-2 mb-6">
            <li>A shoulder driven by thoracic stiffness may improve quickly.</li>
            <li>An irritated rotator cuff typically requires several weeks of treatment.</li>
            <li>A genuinely stiff or frozen shoulder is often measured in months.</li>
          </ul>
          
          <p>Assessment and treatment begin in the first session. Eva will explain what the examination revealed, what is affecting the shoulder, and what the plan involves — including if findings indicate care outside her scope of practice.</p>
          <p>Home exercises often play an essential role. The focus is always on movement quality rather than high repetition counts.</p>

          <hr class="my-12 border-border" />

          <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-12 items-center my-12">
            <div>
              <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-foreground mb-6">Why Scandinavian Clinic</h2>
              <ul class="space-y-4">
                <li><strong>Targeted differential assessment:</strong> Identifying the cause of your shoulder pain. Neck, thoracic spine, ribs, shoulder girdle, and posture are examined as standard.</li>
                <li><strong>Swedish-trained orthopedic manual therapy:</strong> Combines soft-tissue techniques, joint mobilization, rehabilitation, and functional biomechanical assessment.</li>
                <li><strong>Czech-trained in Dynamic Neuromuscular Stabilization:</strong> Trained in the Prague School tradition of Lewit, Janda, and Kolář. <a href="https://scandinavianclinic.com" class="text-primary hover:underline">[Learn more]</a></li>
                <li><strong>Covered by most extended health plans:</strong> As a Registered Massage Therapist, Eva's treatments are eligible for extended health benefits.</li>
              </ul>
              <div class="mt-6">
                <a href="https://scandinavianclinic.com" class="text-primary font-medium hover:underline">See all conditions Eva treats</a>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div>
              <img src={imgFooter} alt="Eva Andersson consultation" class="w-full rounded-xl shadow-lg" />
            </div>
          </div>

          <hr class="my-12 border-border" />

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-foreground mb-8">Frequently asked questions</h2>
          
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div>
              <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-foreground mb-2">Why does my shoulder hurt when I never injured it?</h3>
              <p>Because the shoulder relies heavily on the neck, mid-back, ribs, and shoulder blade to function properly. When those regions don't do their share, the shoulder carries more load than it was designed for. Gradual-onset shoulder pain usually follows this pattern rather than a single traumatic moment.</p>
            </div>
            
            <div>
              <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-foreground mb-2">Why do I need an assessment — isn't shoulder pain just shoulder pain?</h3>
              <p>No. At least a dozen distinct conditions cause shoulder pain, and they require different approaches.</p>
            </div>
            
            <div>
              <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-foreground mb-2">Do I need surgery?</h3>
              <p>Usually not. Structured exercise-based rehabilitation is the recommended first-line approach for most rotator cuff–related shoulder pain, ahead of corticosteroid injections or surgery (Pieters et al., 2020).</p>
            </div>
            
            <div>
              <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-foreground mb-2">Is my rotator cuff torn?</h3>
              <p>It might be, but that may matter less than you think. Rotator cuff changes and asymptomatic tears are very common as we age. A finding on a scan does not automatically prove it is the primary source of your symptoms.</p>
            </div>

            <div>
              <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-foreground mb-2">Why would you treat my mid-back or neck for shoulder pain?</h3>
              <p>Because the arm cannot reach fully overhead without the mid-back extending and rotating, and because neck dysfunction frequently refers pain directly into the shoulder. Overhead reach often improves immediately after freeing up thoracic and rib restrictions.</p>
            </div>
            
            <div>
              <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-foreground mb-2">Why aren't you starting me on rotator cuff strengthening exercises right away?</h3>
              <p>Because loading a joint that isn't tracking properly reinforces the dysfunction that caused the pain. Restoring joint centration and movement quality comes first; strengthening follows.</p>
            </div>
            
            <div>
              <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-foreground mb-2">Can posture really affect my shoulder?</h3>
              <p>Yes. The alignment of your ribcage directly dictates where your shoulder blade rests, changing muscle tension and limiting how far the blade can travel when you lift your arm.</p>
            </div>
            
            <div>
              <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-foreground mb-2">Does massage help shoulder pain?</h3>
              <p>Yes. Relieving tension in overactive muscles around the neck, chest, and shoulder blade is an effective part of treatment when combined with joint mobilization and movement retraining.</p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-accent/10 p-8 rounded-2xl text-center my-16 border border-accent/20">
            <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-foreground mb-4">Schedule your visit</h2>
            <p class="mb-8 max-w-2xl mx-auto">If your shoulder pain developed without an injury, hasn't responded to previous exercise programs, or is limiting your sleep and daily reaching, an assessment can identify what is actually going on.</p>
            <a href="https://booking.scandinavianclinic.com" class="booking-link inline-block bg-accent hover:bg-accent/90 text-accent-foreground font-semibold py-4 px-8 rounded-lg shadow-lg transition-all hover:scale-105 mb-4">Book Your Assessment</a>
            <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">Suite 202 – 101 West 16th Street, North Vancouver · (604) 926-4883</p>
          </div>

          <div class="mt-16 pt-8 border-t border-border/50 text-sm">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-foreground mb-4">References</h3>
            <ol class="list-decimal pl-5 space-y-2 text-muted-foreground/80">
              <li>Karel YHJM, et al. Terminology and diagnostic criteria used in studies investigating patients with subacromial pain syndrome from 1972 to 2019: a scoping review. <em>Physiotherapy</em>. 2023.</li>
              <li>Hegedus EJ, Goode AP, Cook CE, et al. Diagnostic accuracy of clinical tests for subacromial impingement syndrome: a systematic review and meta-analysis. <em>Br J Sports Med</em>. 2012;46(14):964–978.</li>
              <li>Zhong Z, Zang W, Tang Z, et al. Effect of scapular stabilization exercises on subacromial pain (impingement) syndrome: a systematic review and meta-analysis of randomized controlled trials. <em>Frontiers in Neurology</em>. 2024;15:1357763.</li>
              <li>Pieters L, Lewis J, Kuppens K, et al. An update of systematic reviews examining the effectiveness of conservative physical therapy interventions for subacromial shoulder pain. <em>JOSPT</em>. 2020;50(3):131–141.</li>
              <li>Wong CK, Levine WN, Deo K, et al. Natural history of frozen shoulder: fact or fiction? A systematic review. <em>Physiotherapy</em>. 2017;103(1):40–47.</li>
              <li>Kobesová A, Kolář P. Developmental kinesiology: three levels of motor control in the assessment and treatment of the motor system. <em>J Bodyw Mov Ther</em>. 2014;18(1):23–33.</li>
              <li>Frank C, Kobesová A, Kolář P. Dynamic neuromuscular stabilization & sports rehabilitation. <em>Int J Sports Phys Ther</em>. 2013;8(1):62–73.</li>
              <li>Lewit K. <em>Manipulative Therapy: Musculoskeletal Medicine</em>. Churchill Livingstone; 2009.</li>
              <li>Page P, Frank C, Lardner R. <em>Assessment and Treatment of Muscle Imbalance: The Janda Approach</em>. Human Kinetics; 2010.</li>
            </ol>
            <p class="mt-8 text-xs italic text-muted-foreground">Written by Eva Andersson, RMT · Last updated July 2026</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </main>
    
    <footer class="bg-muted py-12 mt-12 border-t border-border">
      <div class="container mx-auto px-4 text-center text-muted-foreground text-sm space-y-4">
        <p>Suite 202 – 101 West 16th Street, North Vancouver, BC V7M 1T3 | <a href="tel:+16049264883" class="hover:text-primary transition-colors">(604) 926-4883</a></p>
        <p>Covered by most extended health insurance plans.</p>
        <p><a href="https://scandinavianclinic.com" class="hover:text-primary underline transition-colors">scandinavianclinic.com</a></p>
      </div>
    </footer>
    
    <ConsentBanner client:load />
    
    <script>
      // Pass URL query parameters to all booking links
      if (typeof window !== 'undefined') {
        const queryParams = new URLSearchParams(window.location.search);
        if (queryParams.toString()) {
          document.querySelectorAll('.booking-link').forEach(el => {
            const link = el as HTMLAnchorElement;
            try {
              const url = new URL(link.href);
              queryParams.forEach((value, key) => {
                url.searchParams.set(key, value);
              });
              link.href = url.toString();
            } catch (e) {
              // Ignore invalid URLs
            }
          });
        }
      }
    </script>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .prose p { margin-bottom: 1.5em; line-height: 1.75; }
